{"round": 1, "phase": "full", "verdict": "ISSUES_FOUND", "blocking": true, "verifier_model": "gpt-5.4", "verifier_provider": "openai", "orchestrator_provider": "anthropic", "findings": [{"description": "`build_envelope()` treats any in-repo path token in suite output as \"implicated\", so `workflow fix` can rewrite unrelated files such as `pytest.ini`.", "severity": "major", "category": "Correctness", "failureScenario": "On this repo's default `python -m pytest` full-suite run, pytest's standard header names the active config file (`configfile: pytest.ini`). `implicated_paths()` scans every path-like token in the entire run output, so `build_envelope()` adds `pytest.ini` to `Envelope.paths` even when no failure touches it. `workflow fix` then passes that envelope to `_confine()`, which will accept a `fix-write path=pytest.ini` block. That is probable on ordinary red suite runs here because the declared suite is pytest and the repo has a `pytest.ini`; it lets a fix round alter runner configuration instead of staying confined to the session diff plus files implicated by the failures.", "evidencePaths": ["ai_router/workflow.py", "ai_router/fixloop.py", "ai_router/agency.py", "ai_router/router-config.yaml", "pytest.ini"], "blocking": true, "section": "body"}, {"description": "**Nit:** `fix()` builds the Copilot CLI grant from `agency.session_scope(repo_root, None, envelope.paths)` (`ai_router/fixloop.py:427-444`, `ai_router/agency.py:177-196`), which adds imported dependencies to the readable scope. So even after the write envelope is narrowed, the prompt still does not literally match the \"receives only failing test names, their output, and the files implicated by the failures\" claim.", "severity": "minor", "blocking": false, "section": "nits"}], "completion_tree": "0539feeee90022f7cc605e6a66b4c41201272191", "recorded_at": "2026-08-27T13:26:13.956719-04:00", "transport": "copilot-cli", "agency": {"mode": "tools", "operations_granted": ["list", "search", "read"], "rea