{"round": 2, "phase": "fix-delta", "verdict": "ISSUES_FOUND", "blocking": true, "verifier_model": "gpt-5.4", "verifier_provider": "openai", "orchestrator_provider": "anthropic", "findings": [{"description": "`_LOCATED` drops common traceback frames, so the real failing implementation file can fall out of the envelope", "severity": "major", "category": "Correctness", "failureScenario": "A normal Python failure still produces a path-based failing-test line such as `FAILED tests/test_add.py::test_adds`, but the only implementation frame is the standard traceback form `File \"app.py\", line 4, in add`. That traceback shape is common in Python code and subprocess failures. `failures()` still identifies the test, but `_LOCATED` ignores the source frame, so `build_envelope()` omits `app.py` and a `fix-write path=app.py` is refused as outside the envelope.", "evidencePaths": ["ai_router/fixloop.py", "ai_router/fixloop.py", "docs/session-sets/148-the-session-framework/project-work-plan.md"], "blocking": true, "section": "body"}, {"description": "The fix round's Copilot read scope is still wider than \u00a73.d: it includes the whole session diff, not just implicated files", "severity": "major", "category": "Correctness", "failureScenario": "On the Copilot CLI path, any ordinary session that has touched an unrelated second file before the full-suite run makes that file readable and counted in-scope, because `build_envelope()` carries the full session diff and `fix()` hands `envelope.paths` to the read grant. That is probable in real sessions, which routinely have code, tests, and session-doc updates in flight together. The generator is therefore explicitly invited to inspect unrelated session files, and the round record will still attest that those reads stayed within scope.", "evidencePaths": ["ai_router/fixloop.py", "ai_router/fixloop.py", "ai_router/agency.py", "ai_router/agency.py", "docs/session-sets/148-the-session-framework/project-work-plan.md"], "blocking": true, "sec